code.code:
async function loadDashboard() {
  const profile = await fetch('/api/profile').then((r) => r.json());
  const notifications = await fetch('/api/notifications').then((r) => r.json());
  const tasks = await fetch('/api/tasks').then((r) => r.json());
  render({ profile, notifications, tasks });
}

scenarios.modelAnswer:
Problems. (1) Request waterfall: three independent operations are awaited one at a time, so the route waits the sum of the latencies instead of the maximum; independent operations should be started before awaiting them. (2) No response.ok check: an HTTP 500 still resolves and its error payload is rendered as data. (3) No cancellation: nothing aborts the fetches when the route unmounts or changes, so the closure keeps working for a screen that no longer exists. (4) No stale-result guard: on fast re-entry, a slower older loadDashboard can resolve after the newer one and overwrite its data, and that happens while the route is mounted, so a mounted check alone would not fix it. (5) No error handling: any rejection is unhandled.

Rewrite:

let currentController: AbortController | undefined;

async function loadDashboard() {
  currentController?.abort();
  const controller = new AbortController();
  currentController = controller;
  const { signal } = controller;

  const responses = await Promise.all([
    fetch('/api/profile', { signal }),
    fetch('/api/notifications', { signal }),
    fetch('/api/tasks', { signal }),
  ]);
  const [profile, notifications, tasks] = await Promise.all(
    responses.map((response) => {
      if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${response.status}`);
      return response.json();
    }),
  );

  if (currentController === controller) {
    render({ profile, notifications, tasks });
  }
}

loadDashboard().catch((error) => {
  if (error.name !== 'AbortError') showError(error);
});

function onRouteCleanup() {
  currentController?.abort();
  currentController = undefined;
}

Why each piece is there: Promise.all is correct because the screen requires every result; it preserves input order and rejects on the first rejection, but that rejection does not cancel the siblings, which is why one AbortSignal is passed to every fetch and aborted on cleanup. AbortError is treated as expected, not surfaced as an error. The currentController identity check is the stale-result guard: only the load that is still current may render, so a slow obsolete response can never overwrite newer data. If partial data were useful, replace Promise.all with Promise.allSettled and render each outcome independently.

code.code:
function debounce(fn: Function, wait: number) {
  let timer: ReturnType<typeof setTimeout> | undefined;
  return function (...args: unknown[]) {
    clearTimeout(timer);
    timer = setTimeout(fn, wait);
  };
}

const onSearch = debounce((query: string) => {
  api.search(query).then(renderResults);
}, 300);
searchInput.addEventListener('input', () => onSearch(searchInput.value));

const onScroll = debounce(() => {
  const y = window.scrollY;
  badge.style.transform = `translateY(${y}px)`;
}, 100);
window.addEventListener('scroll', onScroll);

scenarios.modelAnswer:
Defects. (1) Arguments and this are dropped: the trailing call is setTimeout(fn, wait), so fn runs with no arguments and the searched query is undefined; the collected ...args are thrown away. (2) No cancellation: there is no cancel(), so a pending trailing call cannot be stopped, and on teardown the timer still fires, rendering results for a widget that is gone while the closure retains the stale work. (3) No leading edge or maxWait: a continuous typing burst postpones the search until the burst ends, which fast typists read as lag; the API must specify leading and trailing behavior and whether a maximum wait is required. (4) Wrong tool for the scroll badge: debouncing a visual handler only reduces call count; a timer callback is a task and is not frame-aligned, so the scrollY read and the style write can interleave between frames. (5) The intervals are magic numbers; the right delay is a product decision, not a universal constant.

Production version (trailing edge, argument- and this-forwarding, cancellable):

function debounce<T extends (...args: any[]) => void>(fn: T, wait: number) {
  let timer: ReturnType<typeof setTimeout> | undefined;
  const debounced = function (this: unknown, ...args: Parameters<T>) {
    if (timer) clearTimeout(timer);
    timer = setTimeout(() => fn.apply(this, args), wait);
  };
  debounced.cancel = () => {
    if (timer) clearTimeout(timer);
    timer = undefined;
  };
  return debounced;
}

Contract to document: trailing-edge only unless a leading option is added; maxWait if bursts must be bounded; arguments and this are forwarded; cancel() must be called on component teardown so no trailing call survives unmount. For the scroll badge, keep the handler light and batch the read and the write inside requestAnimationFrame so all reads happen before writes in a frame-aligned callback; a timer alone does not guarantee frame-aligned rendering. Debounce remains the right choice for the search because only the settled query matters; continuous scroll, resize, or pointer work is throttle territory, at most one invocation per interval.

code.code:
type User = { id: string; name: string; role: string };

function mergePages(pages: User[][]): User[] {
  return [...new Set(pages.flat())];
}

scenarios.modelAnswer:
(1) Set membership uses SameValueZero, and for objects that means identity: values are compared by reference, not by structure (MDN Set, https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Set). Each page is parsed separately, so the same user on two pages is two different object literals; neither equals the other, both are kept, and the spread preserves the input order with every duplicate intact. The insertion-order half of the Set reasoning is fine; the equality half is what fails.

(2) Last-wins rewrite keyed by the stable id:

function mergePages(pages: User[][]): User[] {
  const byId = new Map<string, User>();
  for (const page of pages) {
    for (const user of page) {
      byId.set(user.id, user);
    }
  }
  return [...byId.values()];
}

Map.set on an existing key replaces the value, and Map iteration follows the order in which each key was first inserted (MDN Map, https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Map), so the output is last-wins data at first-seen positions. If the rule were first-wins instead, guard with byId.has(user.id) before set, or use the Set-of-ids filter pattern.

(3) Complexity: O(n) average time for n records and O(n) additional space. The question to ask first is the conflict rule: first record wins, last record wins, or merge. It changes the data structure, not just a line, and it should be decided before coding.

code.code:
async function mapWithLimit<T, R>(
  items: readonly T[],
  limit: number,
  task: (item: T) => Promise<R>,
): Promise<R[]> {
  const results: R[] = [];
  let next = 0;

  async function worker() {
    while (true) {
      const index = next++;
      if (index >= items.length) return;
      results.push(await task(items[index]));
    }
  }

  await Promise.all(Array.from({ length: limit }, worker));
  return results;
}

scenarios.modelAnswer:
(1) The order bug: results.push appends in completion order. Workers interleave at every await, so when item 2 resolves after item 5, its result lands after item 5's; whether that happens depends on network timing, which is why the order assertion fails intermittently instead of always. The fix is to store each result at its original index, not at the end of the array.

(2) Silent misbehavior for bad limits: limit = 0 or a negative number spawns zero workers, Promise.all of an empty array resolves immediately, and the function returns an empty array as if the work were done; a non-integer limit is silently truncated. The utility should validate that the limit is a positive integer and throw. The unstated failure contract: Promise.all over the workers rejects on the first worker error, but the other workers' in-flight tasks keep running; they are observed, not cancelled, and their results are never used. A scheduler must state fail-fast versus per-item outcomes, and whether already-started tasks are actively cancelled (thread an AbortSignal) or left to finish.

(3) Fixed version:

async function mapWithLimit<T, R>(
  items: readonly T[],
  limit: number,
  task: (item: T, index: number) => Promise<R>,
): Promise<R[]> {
  if (!Number.isInteger(limit) || limit < 1) throw new RangeError('limit');
  const results = new Array<R>(items.length);
  let next = 0;

  async function worker() {
    while (true) {
      const index = next++;
      if (index >= items.length) return;
      results[index] = await task(items[index], index);
    }
  }

  await Promise.all(
    Array.from({ length: Math.min(limit, items.length) }, worker),
  );
  return results;
}

Guarantees: at most limit tasks are in flight at any moment (the worker pool pulls indices; starting all tasks at once would be the unbounded Promise.all(items.map(task)) pattern this utility exists to prevent); output order equals input order regardless of completion order; the promise rejects on the first task error while already-started tasks are merely observed; invalid limits throw RangeError. The limit protects browser and server resources; it does not guarantee network throughput and does not make individual tasks faster.

code.code:
type RetryOptions = {
  retries?: number;
  baseMs?: number;
  capMs?: number;
  signal?: AbortSignal;
  shouldRetry?: (error: unknown) => boolean;
};

async function retry<T>(
  task: (signal?: AbortSignal) => Promise<T>,
  { retries = 3, baseMs = 200, capMs = 5000, signal, shouldRetry = () => true }: RetryOptions = {},
): Promise<T> {
  for (let attempt = 0; attempt <= retries; attempt++) {
    try {
      return await task(signal);
    } catch (error) {
      if (!shouldRetry(error)) throw error;
      const delay = Math.min(capMs, baseMs * 2 ** attempt) + Math.random() * 100;
      await new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, delay));
    }
  }
  throw new Error('request failed after retries');
}

scenarios.modelAnswer:
(1) Four calls: `attempt` runs 0, 1, 2, 3, so the budget is `retries + 1` attempts — the count is actually correct. The defect is the wait: after the failure at `attempt = 3` there is no attempt left, yet the catch still computes and awaits the exponential delay, which is `min(5000, 200 * 2 ** 3) = 1600` ms plus up to 100 ms of noise. So the user waits up to 1.7 s before a failure the helper already knew was final. Save it as a rule: the delay belongs only between attempts, so it has to be guarded by the same condition that decides whether another attempt exists.

(2) Defects, with the cost of each.
- **Wait after exhaustion**: up to 1.7 s of dead time per failed operation, on top of the three real waits (200, 400, 800 ms maxima). On a retry storm this is the difference between a fast failure and a widget that appears hung.
- **Error identity destroyed**: `throw new Error('request failed after retries')` replaces the last real error — status code, request id, retryability, stack. The caller can no longer distinguish a 503 it may retry later from a 422 it must surface to the user, so logging and error UI both degrade to a string. Reject with the caught error, optionally attaching it via `cause`.
- **The sleep is not cancellable**: the promise ignores `signal`, so an aborted route or unmounted widget keeps the timer pending, and when it fires the helper calls `task` again with an already-aborted signal. The abort has to reject the wait promptly and clear the timer, otherwise cancellation only takes effect one full backoff later.
- **`shouldRetry` defaults to retrying everything**, aborts included. Classification belongs at the caller (the bank says so explicitly), but the helper must at least short-circuit when the signal is already aborted, or a cancelled request becomes a retry loop until the budget runs out.
- **No total budget**: the cap bounds one wait, but nothing bounds the operation. With `retries = 10` the waits alone sum to over 30 s (200 + 400 + 800 + 1600 + 3200 + five capped 5000 ms waits), which no UI deadline survives. A retry policy needs a deadline or a deadline-carrying `AbortSignal` in addition to the per-wait cap.

(3) Rewrite: wait only when another attempt exists, full jitter proportional to the cap, abortable sleep, last error preserved.

```ts
function sleep(ms: number, signal?: AbortSignal): Promise<void> {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    if (signal?.aborted) return reject(signal.reason);
    let timer: ReturnType<typeof setTimeout>;
    const onAbort = () => {
      clearTimeout(timer);
      reject(signal!.reason);
    };
    timer = setTimeout(() => {
      signal?.removeEventListener('abort', onAbort);
      resolve();
    }, ms);
    signal?.addEventListener('abort', onAbort, { once: true });
  });
}

async function retry<T>(
  task: (signal?: AbortSignal) => Promise<T>,
  { retries = 3, baseMs = 200, capMs = 5000, signal, shouldRetry = () => true }: RetryOptions = {},
): Promise<T> {
  for (let attempt = 0; ; attempt++) {
    try {
      return await task(signal);
    } catch (error) {
      if (signal?.aborted) throw error;                                  // an abort is never retryable
      if (attempt >= retries || !shouldRetry(error)) throw error;        // last real error escapes unchanged
      const cap = Math.min(capMs, baseMs * 2 ** attempt);
      await sleep(Math.random() * cap, signal);                          // full jitter in [0, cap)
    }
  }
}
```

Two contract notes for the API surface: the sleep must clear its timer on abort (otherwise the process keeps a pending macrotask and a long-lived tab keeps waking up), and `signal.reason` propagates the caller's own abort value, so aborting with a `DOMException('AbortError')` is what a retryable `catch` at the call site should exclude. MDN documents both surfaces (https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/AbortSignal).

(4) The noise is additive and constant, so it does not scale with the thing it is supposed to shake up. At the first retry the spread is 100 ms against a 200 ms delay (half the window, still useful by accident); by the fourth attempt it is 100 ms against 1600 ms — about 6% — so a cohort that failed together still retries within the same 100 ms window, which is exactly the synchronized wave the jitter was meant to break. Worse, the minimum wait can never fall below `baseMs`, so a blip that clears in 20 ms still costs the user a full 200 ms.

Full jitter is different in kind: it samples uniformly in `[0, cap]`, so the spread *is* the window and grows with the cap, the expected delay halves to `cap / 2`, and a fast recovery can retry almost immediately. The trade-off is visible in the tail: some clients retry very early, which is acceptable because the aggregate arrival rate is what matters, not any single client's pacing. The AWS Architecture Blog post that names the strategy also compares it with equal jitter, `cap / 2 + random(0, cap / 2)`, which keeps a floor at the cost of a smaller spread (https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/architecture/exponential-backoff-and-jitter/).

What the caller still owns: which errors are transient (`shouldRetry`), whether the operation is safe to repeat at all, and the deadline. A retry loop cannot make a non-idempotent POST safe, and no amount of jitter helps a request the user already navigated away from.

code.code:
function deepEqual(a: unknown, b: unknown): boolean {
  if (a === b) return true;
  if (typeof a !== 'object' || typeof b !== 'object' || a === null || b === null) {
    return false;
  }
  const ak = Object.keys(a);
  const bk = Object.keys(b);
  if (ak.length !== bk.length) return false;
  return ak.every((key) => deepEqual((a as any)[key], (b as any)[key]));
}

scenarios.modelAnswer:
(1) One concrete input per defect, with the direction of the error.
- **False negative — `===` is not SameValue.** `deepEqual(NaN, NaN)` returns `false`, and so does `{ v: NaN }` versus `{ v: NaN }`. A store that republishes a `NaN` therefore looks changed on every publish, which is the worst failure mode for a memo guard: it quietly disables the memoization it was supposed to protect. `Object.is` fixes this step.
- **Undocumented choice on signed zero.** `deepEqual(0, -0)` returns `true`. Either answer can be defensible, but the function has made the decision silently; if `-0` encodes something in the store, the change is invisible and the panel never updates.
- **False positive — no prototype check.** `deepEqual([], {})` returns `true`, and so does `new Map([['a', 1]])` versus `new Map()` and two Dates with different instants. `Object.keys` reports own enumerable strings, and these values keep their payload in internal slots or in an own non-enumerable `length`, so an array and a record with the same (empty) key list are declared interchangeable. Matching prototypes before keys is what separates `[]` from `{}` and a class instance from a plain record; built-ins then need their own branches, because no key walk can see a Map's entries.
- **False positive — inspecting one side by count and reading the other through the prototype chain.** `deepEqual({ x: 1 }, Object.assign(Object.create({ x: 1 }), { y: undefined }))` returns `true`: `ak.length === bk.length` passes (one key each), `a.x` is compared against `b`'s *inherited* `x`, and `b`'s own `y` is never inspected. `Object.keys` also drops symbol and non-enumerable state, so a store keyed by `Symbol` compares as equal to an empty object. The fix is keys that are own and enumerable on **both** sides — but not a blanket `Reflect.ownKeys` walk: an array also owns a non-enumerable `length`, so a strict enumerability guard applied to raw own keys rejects every pair of equal arrays, which is precisely what the bank's skeleton does (`deepEqual([1, 2], [1, 2])` is `false`).

(2) `const a: any = { name: 'order' }; a.self = a;` and the same shape on the right. The roots are distinct objects, so the `a === b` shortcut does not fire; both have `['name', 'self']`; comparing `a.self` with `b.self` recurses into the very pair that is already being compared, and nothing records that. The runtime raises `RangeError: Maximum call stack size exceeded` — not a hang, a crash inside the selector. Normalized stores, `parent` back-references and anything built from a DOM-adjacent graph legitimately contain cycles.

(3) Rewrite. The contract is written into the code, in the order it must be checked.

```ts
type Seen = WeakMap<object, WeakSet<object>>;

function deepEqual(a: unknown, b: unknown, seen: Seen = new WeakMap()): boolean {
  if (Object.is(a, b)) return true;                       // NaN equals NaN; +0 and -0 differ
  if (typeof a !== 'object' || typeof b !== 'object' || a === null || b === null) return false;

  if (Object.getPrototypeOf(a) !== Object.getPrototypeOf(b)) return false; // [] is not {}

  const left = a as object, right = b as object;
  let compared = seen.get(left);
  if (compared?.has(right)) return true;                  // this pair is already in progress
  if (!compared) seen.set(left, (compared = new WeakSet()));
  compared.add(right);

  if (left instanceof Date) return Object.is(left.getTime(), (right as Date).getTime());
  if (left instanceof Map || left instanceof Set) return compareCollections(left, right as never, seen);

  const enumerable = (o: object) =>
    Reflect.ownKeys(o).filter((k) => Object.prototype.propertyIsEnumerable.call(o, k));
  const ak = enumerable(left), bk = enumerable(right);    // own enumerable: keeps array `length` out
  return ak.length === bk.length && ak.every((key) =>
    Object.prototype.propertyIsEnumerable.call(right, key) &&
    deepEqual((left as Record<PropertyKey, unknown>)[key],
              (right as Record<PropertyKey, unknown>)[key], seen));
}
```

The contract it states, line by line: `NaN` equals `NaN`; `+0` and `-0` are different; non-objects compare by SameValue with no coercion; prototypes must match before any key is looked at, which is what separates `[]` from `{}` and a class instance from a plain record; keys are own **enumerable** string and symbol keys on both sides, and filtering by enumerability is what keeps an array's non-enumerable `length` out of the comparison; Dates compare instants. Maps and Sets cannot be covered by a key walk — their entries live in internal slots, so without a branch every Map equals every other one — and the branch is `compareCollections`:

```ts
function compareCollections(
  left: Map<unknown, unknown> | Set<unknown>,
  right: Map<unknown, unknown> | Set<unknown>,
  seen: Seen,
): boolean {
  if (left.size !== right.size) return false;
  if (left instanceof Set) {
    const other = right as Set<unknown>;
    // object members need a structural search: O(n²) unless you hash them
    return [...left].every((value) => [...other].some((candidate) => deepEqual(value, candidate, seen)));
  }
  const map = right as Map<unknown, unknown>;
  for (const [key, value] of left) {
    if (!map.has(key) || !deepEqual(value, map.get(key), seen)) return false; // keys keep Map identity
  }
  return true;
}
```

Note what that decides: Map *keys* keep Map's own SameValueZero identity rule, while *values* are compared structurally. Structural key comparison is the other defensible contract, and it needs a canonical hash to stay near-linear; Sets of object members have the same problem, and the naive member search above is O(n²) per collection, which is exactly the kind of cost a selector cannot pay by accident.

**The limitation of the cycle handling**: recording a pair and treating a revisit as equal is a bisimulation choice — "these graphs unfold the same way forever". It terminates and it is what Node's and lodash's comparisons do, but it makes a one-node cycle equal to a two-node cycle. That is usually the answer people want; it is still a decision the contract should state rather than a universal truth. The alternative — tracking pairs only on the current comparison stack and removing them on unwind — terminates too, and differs precisely on those graphs.

(4) Because its cost is the whole graph, on every invocation, in the one place where cost is multiplied by interaction rate: a selector re-runs on each store change, and a deep comparison there turns a cheap identity check into a full walk — with Set or Map members compared naively, quadratic inside the collection. It also hides the real invalidation signal: if the panel needs to re-render when an order's status changes, comparing `status` (or `id` + `updatedAt`, or a version counter bumped by the store) says that directly, in constant time, and cannot be fooled by an accidental deep equality. Deep equality earns its cost in tests and one-shot questions, not in a render path — which is the same conclusion the bank draws when it recommends stable identities, version numbers and normalized entities over deep comparison in hot loops.
